<section xml:id="section-galois">
  <title>Basic Ideas of Galois Theory</title>
    <p>
      In this last section of these notes, I want to give you just a
      glimpse of some of the major directions in the theory of fields.
      I'm going to start with Galois theory, which is a celebrated
      piece of mathematics whose development is deeply embedded in the
      whole history of abstract algebra. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Galois theory is a theory of field extensions. I need a few
      definitions to give an outline of the ideas that go into Galois
      theory. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A field extension <m>K \rightarrow L</m> is called a
          <term>normal extension</term> if it is the splitting field
          of finitely many polynomials over <m>K</m>. Equivalently,
          if a polynomial in <m>K[x]</m> has a root in <m>L</m>, then
          it must factor completely into linear factors in
          <m>L[x]</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>K</m> be a field. A polynomials <m>p(x) \in K[x]</m>
          is called <term>separable</term> is it has no repeated roots
          in any field extension of <m>K</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A field extension <m>K \rightarrow </m> is called a
          <term>separable extension</term> if it isomorphic to
          <m>K[x]/\langle p(x) \rangle</m> for <m>p(x)</m> an
          irreducible separable polynomial.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A isormophism from a field to itself is called an
          <term>automorphism</term> of the field. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      This is the setup. Basically, we are figuring out a nice set of
      polynomials and finite extension that behaviour reasonable well.
      We are particularly avoiding polynomials with repeated factors,
      since extensions with repeated factors are a bit strange:
      attaching one root is sufficient to factor the repeated factor.
      The extra repeated factors don't really add much to the field
      extension and confuse the technical details. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Then here is the main definition.  
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A field extension <m>K \rightarrow L</m> is a <term>Galois
          extension</term> if it the splitting field of a separable
          polynomial. This is equivalent to it being both normal and
          separable. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Alright, what is interesting about Galois extension? The
      automorphisms are interesting. Galois theory is all about
      understanding field extensions by their automorphisms. In a
      Galois extension, various roots have been attached to the field:
      all of roots of the separable polynomial. I can identify a set
      of these new roots added to the field. 
      <md>
        \{\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \ldots, \alpha_n\}
      </md>
      The key idea of Galois theory is that the automorphisms of a
      Galois extension <m>L</m> which preserve <m>K</m> are entirely
      determined by permutations of this set of roots. This is encoded
      as a group. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>K \rightarrow L</m> be a Galois extension. Then the
          Galois group <m>\text{Gal}(L/K)</m> is the group of automorphism
          of <m>L</m> which act trivially on <m>K</m>. It is a finite
          group and can be realized as a subgroup of <m>S_n</m> by
          consider the premutations of the roots of the separable
          polynomial which defines the extension. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Galois theory takes something complicated and usually infinite
      (a bunch of field extensions) and assigns a finite permutation
      group to is (the Galois group). Then it proceeds to prove that
      the Galois groups has all of the possible information about all
      fields that sit between <m>K</m> and <m>L</m>. The complicated
      structure of all these fields is reduced to the structure of one
      finite group. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Galois theory is famous for precisely this reasons: it's of the
      earliest examples of a powerful reduction in complexity. There
      is a clear, finite, calculatable and understandable entity which
      completely classifying a much more complicated situation. You
      want to understand these fields? All you need is the Galois
      group. 
    </p>
    <p>
      It's also a powerful relationship between field theory and group
      theory. It changes problems in field theory to problems in group
      theory in a way that doesn't lose any information. As such, it
      is also the archetype of a common pattern in modern mathematics:
      of uncovering deep and powerful connections between branches of
      mathematics. There is a deep and important theory that proves
      that all finite group actually show up as Galois groups of some
      field extension. Therefore, you can do all of finite groups
      theory in the context of field extensions if you wish, in
      addition to studying any Galois extension via group theory. This
      way of connecting disparate piece of mathematics eventually gets
      encoded into the extremely abstract but amazing branch of
      mathematics called Category Theory. Finite groups and field
      extensions are both <sq>categories</sq> and Galois theory is a
      perfect matching between these two categories. 
    </p>
</section>
